WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.270
- [Man] What if I told you dogs
don't care about us at all?

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.589
- That would make me so sad. What?

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:10.172
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:18.954
- Before I got Murphy, I
talked about getting a dog

00:00:18.954 --> 00:00:21.081
for two years straight.

00:00:21.081 --> 00:00:24.184
- I decided to just walk into
the animal shelter one day.

00:00:24.184 --> 00:00:26.135
- Louis was the only one next to the mom

00:00:26.135 --> 00:00:28.616
so I could tell he was the
youngest and the shyest.

00:00:28.616 --> 00:00:31.083
- And within five minutes we saw Marty

00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:32.697
and we signed the papers.

00:00:32.697 --> 00:00:35.749
- I think I remember one
time I just had a breakdown

00:00:35.749 --> 00:00:38.909
and she just walked
over and kissed my face

00:00:38.909 --> 00:00:41.427
and I was like, "You know, this is okay."

00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:42.260
(laughs)

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.759
- We call him the joy of our lives.

00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:45.445
- I was in a wheel chair for a little bit

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:48.944
and having her sit with
me in my bed every day

00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:51.295
when I was in recovery
was the greatest comfort.

00:00:51.295 --> 00:00:52.481
- Everyone I was really close

00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:54.627
with started moving away one by one.

00:00:54.627 --> 00:00:56.109
I never had a chance to be really lonely

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:57.935
because I always had Louis with me.

00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:00.255
- [Man] So would you say
that you love Murphy?

00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:01.088
- Yeah.

00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:02.343
- Yes. I love Ella.

00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:04.862
- [Man] And do you feel
like Ella loves you?

00:01:04.862 --> 00:01:06.371
- Yeah definitely.

00:01:06.371 --> 00:01:08.122
- I think Louis loves me, yeah.

00:01:08.122 --> 00:01:10.008
- [Man] What if I told
you that there was a lot

00:01:10.008 --> 00:01:12.075
of scientific thought that dogs

00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:13.879
don't actually care about us at all?

00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:14.924
- Uh.

00:01:14.924 --> 00:01:17.341
- What? What are the details?

00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:19.714
- [Man] Well we can get to those.

00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:22.297
(upbeat music)

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.762
- They're little bone shaped.

00:01:25.762 --> 00:01:28.178
Bad news comes in cute
packages, apparently.

00:01:28.178 --> 00:01:29.011
(laughs)

00:01:29.011 --> 00:01:30.895
- So it says in the 19th century,

00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:33.263
naturalist Charles
Darwin traveled the world

00:01:33.263 --> 00:01:36.044
on the HMS Beagle,
leading to the formulation

00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:37.529
of his theory of evolution.

00:01:37.529 --> 00:01:40.345
- Darwin leader wrote
extensively about the possibility

00:01:40.345 --> 00:01:44.128
that dogs have rich emotional
lives, but couldn't prove it.

00:01:44.128 --> 00:01:46.650
- The true modern era
of dog research began

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.925
with scientist, Ivan Pavlov.

00:01:48.925 --> 00:01:50.942
Pavlov is most famous for his discovery

00:01:50.942 --> 00:01:53.357
that dogs who had heard a
bell and were given food

00:01:53.357 --> 00:01:56.916
eventually began to salivate
merely at the sound of the bell

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.145
even when no food was present.

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:01.200
- This gave rise to behaviorism

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.231
- And the belief among
the scientific community

00:02:03.231 --> 00:02:05.169
that dogs are merely robotic creatures

00:02:05.169 --> 00:02:07.068
- Who give us affection
not because they love us

00:02:07.068 --> 00:02:09.500
but because they know it will
lead us to giving them food.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.500
- Marty, do you love me?

00:02:13.882 --> 00:02:15.155
Looks away.

00:02:15.155 --> 00:02:17.592
- And so for a century, this view held.

00:02:17.592 --> 00:02:19.091
Dogs were not our best friends,

00:02:19.091 --> 00:02:21.732
they were just what one
science writer called,

00:02:21.732 --> 00:02:23.985
"Con artist at the very
top of profession."

00:02:23.985 --> 00:02:28.060
Simply put, dogs did not
and could not love us.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.757
That makes me really sad
because I love you so much.

00:02:31.757 --> 00:02:33.107
- Murphy you're a liar?

00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:34.826
- I'm hoping this is not true though.

00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:37.440
- I feel like something is coming.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.023
(upbeat music)

00:02:40.923 --> 00:02:42.804
(clicks)

00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:45.921
- Hi this is Dr. Gregory
Berns, I'm a neuroscientist

00:02:45.921 --> 00:02:49.103
in Atlanta, Georgia and
I always wanted to know

00:02:49.103 --> 00:02:52.801
whether dogs love us like
the way we love them.

00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:54.053
But of course they can't tell us

00:02:54.053 --> 00:02:56.533
so I had to figure out a
different way to do it.

00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.971
I used MRI and trained
my dog to go in an MRI

00:03:00.971 --> 00:03:03.922
so I could look at her brain
and see what she's thinking

00:03:03.922 --> 00:03:06.063
and feeling while she interacted with me.

00:03:06.063 --> 00:03:10.488
And pretty soon we had a group
of a dozen dogs doing MRI's.

00:03:10.488 --> 00:03:11.781
And one of the things that we found

00:03:11.781 --> 00:03:14.097
was that the reward
center of a dog's brain

00:03:14.097 --> 00:03:17.629
responds to the sound of the human's voice

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:19.466
praising them as much

00:03:19.466 --> 00:03:22.832
and in many cases more than food itself.

00:03:22.832 --> 00:03:25.880
And what this shows is
that dogs really do care

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.899
about the social bond as much

00:03:28.899 --> 00:03:32.008
and in many cases more than
just the provision of food.

00:03:32.008 --> 00:03:34.654
I think like many dog owners realize

00:03:34.654 --> 00:03:37.385
is that they're amazing
creatures with kind of

00:03:37.385 --> 00:03:41.226
the depth of emotion that goes beyond

00:03:41.226 --> 00:03:42.876
what scientists used to think

00:03:42.876 --> 00:03:45.941
in that they are very
much capable of love.

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:48.599
It's certainly in their
way and very much like

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:50.440
we humans love each other too.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.190
- Awe. I'm gonna cry.

00:03:54.213 --> 00:03:55.046
- Oh.

00:03:56.359 --> 00:03:58.074
You do love me Murph?

00:03:58.074 --> 00:03:59.891
- Marty, did you hear that?

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:02.160
- That makes me really happy.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.010
- I feel like I can
sort of more freely say

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.955
that she is also my best friend now.

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:09.376
I'm sorry boyfriend but
my best friend's my dog.

00:04:09.376 --> 00:04:10.331
(laughing)

00:04:10.331 --> 00:04:12.865
- I'm really glad that
our feelings are mutual

00:04:12.865 --> 00:04:16.074
and I hope Louis loves me forever.

00:04:16.074 --> 00:04:19.074
(happy music plays)

00:04:26.385 --> 00:04:28.718
(whooshing)

